---
export const prerender = false

import PackClosing from '../../components/packs/PackClosing.astro'
import PackEyebrow from '../../components/packs/PackEyebrow.astro'
import PackHeading from '../../components/packs/PackHeading.astro'
import PackHero from '../../components/packs/PackHero.astro'
import PackIntro from '../../components/packs/PackIntro.astro'
import PackLayout from '../../components/packs/PackLayout.astro'
import PackLifecycle from '../../components/packs/PackLifecycle.astro'
import PackProse from '../../components/packs/PackProse.astro'
import PackSection from '../../components/packs/PackSection.astro'
import PackSectionCta from '../../components/packs/PackSectionCta.astro'

const productSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Product',
  name: 'Operator for Law Firms',
  description:
    'The Operator is a worker you hire, not software you buy. For a personal injury practice it works inside your case management system and carries a case from records to settlement: cited medical chronologies, the billing picture, first drafts for your attorneys, and settled cases tracked until the money goes out. You set the lines it works within, and it never signs on its own.',
  brand: { '@type': 'Brand', name: 'SMD Services' },
  offers: {
    '@type': 'Offer',
    availability: 'https://schema.org/InStock',
    seller: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'SMD Services' },
  },
}

// Section 04: the lifecycle walk. One stretch of a personal injury case (from the
// records arriving to the money going out) carried start to finish, each step in the
// standard's three-line shape (Operator does / your part / if it stalls). Generic and
// brand-free: the shape of plaintiff PI work, not any one firm's tools or rules. The
// section framing (illustrative + the fail-closed honesty in section 07) carries the
// truthfulness, per the kit-grammar guard. PI leads this pack by Captain decision
// 2026-09-14 (docs/marketing/pack-standard.md amendment of that date).
const lifecycleSteps = [
  {
    title: 'The records arrive',
    operator:
      'Notices new medical records and bills as they land in the case, logs what came in, and checks it against what was ordered.',
    your: 'Nothing.',
    stalls: 'Flags a provider whose records have not arrived.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The chronology',
    operator:
      'When someone asks, reads the records into a chronology: every visit, diagnosis, and treatment in order, each entry cited to the page it came from, every claim checked against that page. Files it back into the case with the records it relied on.',
    your: 'Read it, and use it.',
    stalls:
      'Says plainly what it could not read, like a missing bill or an unreadable scan, instead of guessing.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The billing picture',
    operator:
      'Pulls the provider bills into one worksheet, reconciled to the records, and marks it incomplete where a bill is missing.',
    your: 'Review the totals.',
    stalls: 'Lists the bills still outstanding.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The demand',
    operator:
      'Prepares a first draft of the demand from the record and the chronology, and routes it to the attorney.',
    your: 'The attorney judges the case, sets the number, and finishes the letter.',
    stalls: 'Reminds the attorney the draft is waiting.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Mediation and case review',
    operator:
      'Prepares first drafts of the documents a case needs along the way, like a mediation brief or a case evaluation memo, from the same record.',
    your: 'The attorney decides what goes in, what comes out, and what gets signed.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The deadlines',
    operator:
      'Reads the dates a case depends on, proposes the deadline where one has to be worked out, and asks the attorney to confirm.',
    your: 'Confirm or correct. The confirmation is logged with your name and the time.',
    stalls: 'Reminds before the date.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Settlement to disbursement',
    operator:
      'Keeps a working list of settled cases and the provider balances still open on each, so nothing sits unnoticed between a settlement and a payout.',
    your: 'Negotiate the balances and disburse.',
    stalls: 'Surfaces the settled case that has gone quiet.',
  },
  {
    title: 'What needs you',
    operator:
      'Sends one short summary of what needs attention: a draft waiting, a deadline near, a balance still open.',
    your: 'React to the few items.',
    stalls: 'Re-surfaces anything missed.',
  },
]
---

<PackLayout
  title="Operator for Law Firms | SMD Services"
  description="A worker you hire, not software you buy. For personal injury firms, the Operator carries a case from records to settlement: cited chronologies, the billing picture, first drafts for your attorneys, and settled cases tracked to payout. It starts with an assessment."
  schema={productSchema}
>
  <!-- 01 Hero -->
  <PackHero slug="law-firm">
    <Fragment slot="heading">The Case, From Records To Settlement.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      The Operator is a worker you hire, not software you buy. For a personal injury practice it
      works inside your case management system and carries the heavy middle of every case: the
      medical records, the chronology, the billing picture, the first drafts, and the settled cases
      still waiting to pay out. You set the lines it works within, like anything bound for opposing
      counsel or the court, and it never signs on its own.
    </Fragment>
  </PackHero>

  <!-- 02 The problem -->
  <PackSection bg="white">
    <PackEyebrow>The Problem</PackEyebrow>
    <PackHeading variant="prose">Every Case Runs On Records.</PackHeading>
    <PackProse>
      <p>
        A personal injury case is only as strong as its medical records, and there are a lot of
        them: visits, imaging, therapy, bills, often thousands of pages for one client. Before
        anyone can value the case, write the demand, or prepare for mediation, someone has to read
        all of it and put it in order.
      </p>
      <p>
        That reading is slow, it is expensive to send out, and it never stops arriving. Meanwhile
        settled cases wait on provider balances before the money can go out, and the attorneys have
        more drafting in front of them than hours. None of it is legal judgment. All of it stands
        between your attorneys and the work only they can do.
      </p>
    </PackProse>
  </PackSection>

  <!-- 03 What the Operator does -->
  <PackSection bg="background">
    <PackEyebrow>What It Does</PackEyebrow>
    <PackHeading variant="prose">An Always-On Member Of The Case Team.</PackHeading>
    <PackProse>
      <p>
        The Operator works inside your case management system, where your records already land. Its
        job is to carry the heavy middle of a case so your people do not have to: it reads the
        records, builds the chronology, pulls the bills together, prepares first drafts, and keeps
        track of what still stands between a settlement and a payout.
      </p>
      <p>
        When it needs a person, it comes to them with the work already prepared, so their part is
        usually a read, a correction, or a yes. Every piece of work product goes to an attorney to
        review and finish. It never signs on its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        It does not replace your drafting tools or your records vendors. It takes what they deliver,
        does the work between them, and puts the result back where your team already looks.
      </p>
    </PackProse>
  </PackSection>

  <!-- 04 The lifecycle, step by step -->
  <PackSection bg="white">
    <PackEyebrow>The Lifecycle</PackEyebrow>
    <PackHeading variant="grid">One Case, Step By Step.</PackHeading>
    <PackIntro>
      Here is one stretch of a case's life, from the records arriving to the money going out,
      carried start to finish. This is the shape of the work, not a fixed script; we build it around
      how your firm actually runs.
    </PackIntro>
    <PackLifecycle steps={lifecycleSteps} />
  </PackSection>

  <!-- 05 In use today: the proof, routed to the case study -->
  <PackSection bg="background">
    <PackEyebrow>In Use Today</PackEyebrow>
    <PackHeading variant="prose">Already Carrying Real Cases.</PackHeading>
    <PackProse>
      <p>
        The walk above is the shape of the work. Here is some of it in practice: for a plaintiff
        personal injury firm, the Operator has delivered 18 medical chronologies from more than
        29,000 pages of records, along with first drafts of a demand letter, a case evaluation memo,
        and a mediation brief.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a
          href="/case-studies/personal-injury-law-firm"
          data-ev="law-firm-case-study"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-[color:var(--ss-color-primary)] hover:text-[color:var(--ss-color-primary-hover)]"
        >
          <span>Read the case study</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </p>
    </PackProse>
  </PackSection>

  <!-- 06 How it connects -->
  <PackSection bg="white">
    <PackEyebrow>How It Connects</PackEyebrow>
    <PackHeading variant="prose">Through The Systems You Already Run.</PackHeading>
    <PackProse>
      <p>
        Your records vendors already deliver into your case management system, and your attorneys
        already draft in the tools they trust. The Operator works in the same place: it reads what
        lands in the case, puts its chronologies and drafts back into the case, and routes them to
        the right person. Nothing has to be emailed around or re-uploaded by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        It works on your own accounts, inside the systems you already pay for. It is the connective
        tissue between them, not another system to learn.
      </p>
    </PackProse>
    <PackSectionCta slug="law-firm" />
  </PackSection>

  <!-- 07 Quiet by design -->
  <PackSection bg="background">
    <PackEyebrow>Quiet By Design</PackEyebrow>
    <PackHeading variant="prose">It Takes Load Off. It Does Not Add It.</PackHeading>
    <PackProse>
      <p>
        The Operator is built to reduce the noise, not add to it. Routine items are gathered into a
        single short summary rather than a stream of notifications, and it reaches out directly only
        when something genuinely needs a person. The goal is that your team feels work coming off
        their plate, not one more thing pinging them.
      </p>
    </PackProse>
  </PackSection>

  <!-- 08 The line, and what we prove first -->
  <PackSection bg="white">
    <PackEyebrow>The Line</PackEyebrow>
    <PackHeading variant="prose">The Lawyer's Work Stays The Lawyer's.</PackHeading>
    <PackProse spaced>
      <p>
        The Operator does the reading, the organizing, and the first draft, and brings it to a
        lawyer. The judgment stays with your attorneys: what a case is worth, the strategy, the call
        on a matter, what gets signed. You set the lines it works within, like anything bound for
        opposing counsel or the court, and you can move them. Trust balances are read, never moved.
        Every action it takes is logged where you can see it.
      </p>
      <p>
        And we are honest about what we prove before we rely on it. The parts that depend on reading
        your records and matching your firm's rules, like a date read off a document or a bill
        matched to a visit, we validate on your real cases first. Where accuracy is not yet proven,
        the Operator surfaces what it found and asks, rather than acting on its own. It earns more
        autonomy only as it proves accurate on your work. The configuration, the logs, and the off
        switch stay with you.
      </p>
    </PackProse>
  </PackSection>

  <!-- 09 Close -->
  <PackClosing slug="law-firm">
    <Fragment slot="pitch">
      We learn how your firm actually runs a case, find where the time goes and where things slip,
      and shape the Operator to fit: what it reads, what it drafts, how much it does on its own,
      where a person stays in the loop. If it is not the right fit, we will tell you.
    </Fragment>
  </PackClosing>
</PackLayout>
